[Step 600] Q: If the odd function $f(x) = x^2 \cdot \sin x + c - 3$ has a domain of $[a+2, b] (b > a+2)$, then find the value of $a + b + c = $\_\_\_\_\_\_.
Ground truth: 1
Response[0]:
<start_working_out>
Since $f(x)$ is an odd function, $\sin (-x) = -\sin x$, then we can get
\begin{align*}
f(-x) &=-x^2 \cdot \sin x + c - 3 \\
&= -f(x)
\end{align*}
\begin{align*}
x^2 \cdot \sin x -c + 3 & =f(x)
\end{align*}
from $f(-x)=-f(x)$.
Because the above equation is symmetrical, $-c+3=0$ and $f(x)$ must be the even function. But the domain is $[a+2, b]$ ($b > a+2$), if $f(x)$ is an even f
